-- title:
--   Shifting and shrinking subsets of a normed space
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a normed additive group. For a subset $S \subseteq E$ and $r \in \mathbb{R}$, the **$r$-shrinking** and, for $x \in E$, the **shift** are
--
--   $$S_r \;=\; \big\{y \in S \ :\ \bar B(y,r) \subseteq S\big\}, \qquad S[x] \;=\; \{y \in E \ :\ y - x \in S\} \;=\; S + x .$$
--
--   The shrinking keeps the points of $S$ that are at distance at least $r$ from its complement; applied to a double cone it yields the double half-cone of the source's Definition 2.1. The shift is translation of a set, in the form in which a cone based at a point is written.
--
--   Both operations use only the normed group structure — no inner product, no finite dimension — so they are developed at that generality, which is also the generality in which the geometric lemma about cubes and shrunk cones is proved. The interplay between them is what the surrounding arguments exploit: a shrunk set is the intersection of the shifts of the original over a ball of shift vectors, and conversely the union of the shifts of a doubly-shrunk set over that ball is contained in the singly-shrunk set.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No positivity hypothesis is placed on $r$: for $r \le 0$ the shrinking is just $S$ itself (up to the degenerate closed ball), so the intended reading requires $r > 0$ at the use sites.
